rachel: Quick heads-up  PR #1045 adds a compact troubleshooting card for 429/timeouts/context-limit that product can embed in support KBs. Wanted a quick tone + safety check on the fallback copy and the JS snippet.

omar: link?

rachel: https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/pull/1045 (WIP). Changes: condensed card, 3-action checklist (detect, respect Retry-After, fallback), and a tiny client-side snippet that prefers cached fallback for interactive flows.

tomas: skimmed  looks good. please ensure the fallback text doesn't promise results we can't guarantee. short phrasing suggestion: "We returned a cached summary due to capacity limits; try again later for an updated response."

elaine: nit: the snippet uses `timeout_ms` in one place and `deadline` elsewhere. standardize to `deadline`. Also add a one-liner about honoring `Retry-After` when present.

rachel: good catches. updated to `deadline` and added header handling. here's the compact snippet I put in the card:```js
// compact pattern for interactive clients: trim -> try -> fallback
async function queryWithFallback(client, prompt, budget) {
  const trimmed = truncateToBudget(prompt, budget);
  try {
    const res = await client.request({ model: 'redwood-qa', input: trimmed, deadline: 3000 });
    return { ok: true, text: res.output };
  } catch (err) {
    const ra = normalizeHeader(err.headers, 'retry-after');
    if (ra) return { ok: false, retryAfter: Number(ra) };
    const cached = await readCachedSummary(prompt);
    if (cached) return { ok: false, fallback: cached, message: 'Returned cached summary due to capacity' };
    throw err;
  }
}
```

tomas: looks tidy. can we add a single QA step in the doc: "simulate 429 with Retry-After and confirm client surface value" so support can validate integrations?

elaine: also propose adding a short test file in examples/ that mocks a 429 with Retry-After and asserts the snippet returns retryAfter. saves future breakages.

rachel: plan: I'll add the QA note and open a follow-up issue to add the mock test. docs-bot, can you lint the card and run the accessibility quick-scan?

